(Bug #41's class through bug #87's spelling. A `value` that took the
 buffer's struct pointer was not merely wrong on the first read - it was
 tied to the buffer for life, so refilling the buffer rewrote the `value`
 and resizing it left the `value` pointing at freed memory. The copy has
 to be independent, which is why #51 fixed `as text` by copying rather
 than by adding an offset.

 The type checks are here for the mirror of #51's trap: a buffer flowing
 into a `value` is a CONVERSION, not a retype, so the name must still be a
 `value` afterwards - `Text (dynamic)`, dynamic being the word that says
 the tag is read at runtime - and not be relabelled a buffer.)

To 'keep a copy' with a buffer called incoming.
  a value called kept is incoming.
  Clear incoming.
  append "changed" to incoming.
  Print kept.

(clear and refill: the snapshot must not follow the buffer)
a buffer called source is 64 bytes in size.
append "first" to source.
a value called snapshot is source.
Clear source.
append "second" to source.
Print snapshot.
Print source.
Print snapshot's type.

(resize: the half that would otherwise be a use-after-free)
a buffer called growing is "abc".
a value called before is growing.
append "defghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz" to growing.
Print before.
Print growing.

(a frame-local copy inside a function is a different store in codegen)
a buffer called handed is 64 bytes in size.
append "first" to handed.
'keep a copy' with handed.
Print handed.
